window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.3","title":"Bayesian and Frequentist Thinking: Two Ways to Learn from Data","goal":"Update a belief about a checkout page's conversion rate (14 buyers out of 40) with a Beta prior, get a 95% credible interval, and compare it with the usual confidence interval.","question":"An online shop shows its new checkout page to 40 customers and 14 buy (35%). The old page converted 25% of customers. Is the new page really better, or did 40 customers just happen to buy a bit more? We answer it the Bayesian way, with a probability for the true rate, and compare it with the usual frequentist answer.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Prior, data and posterior","text":"A Beta(a, b) prior acts like a past record of a successes and b failures. Beta(1, 1) is flat: every rate is equally plausible. After k buyers out of n, the posterior is simply Beta(a + k, b + n - k).","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\na, b = 1, 1\nk, n = 14, 40\n\npost_a, post_b = a + k, b + n - k\nposterior = stats.beta(post_a, post_b)\n\nprint(f'posterior = Beta({post_a}, {post_b})')\nprint(f'posterior mean = {posterior.mean():.3f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, used later for a square root.","Load matplotlib's plotting tools as plt, for the final picture.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which hold the Beta distribution.","The prior Beta(1, 1): a flat starting belief, as if we had seen 1 success and 1 failure. Every rate from 0 to 1 is equally plausible.","The data: 14 buyers (k) out of 40 customers (n).","Bayesian update: the posterior is Beta(prior successes + k, prior failures + non-buyers). We just add the counts.","Create the posterior Beta distribution as an object we can ask questions of.","Print the posterior's two numbers, 15 and 27.","Print the posterior mean, our best single estimate of the true conversion rate."],"meaning":"Starting from a flat prior, the data turn it into Beta(15, 27). Its mean is 0.357, a touch above the observed 35%, because the prior adds one pretend success and one pretend failure. The posterior is our updated belief about the true conversion rate.","out":"posterior = Beta(15, 27)\nposterior mean = 0.357","figs":[]},{"title":"Credible interval and a direct probability","text":"A 95% credible interval takes the middle 95% of the posterior. We can also read off the probability that the new page beats the old 25% rate, which a p-value cannot give directly.","code":"low, high = posterior.ppf([0.025, 0.975])\np_better = posterior.sf(0.25)\n\nprint(f'95% credible interval: {low:.3f} to {high:.3f}')\nprint(f'P(rate > 25%) = {p_better:.3f}')","explain":["ppf finds the points that cut off 2.5% on each side, giving the middle 95% of the posterior: the 95% credible interval.","sf (survival function) gives the posterior probability that the true rate is above 0.25. A p-value cannot say this directly.","Print the credible interval.","Print the probability that the new page beats the old 25%."],"meaning":"The 95% credible interval runs from 0.221 to 0.506: given this data and prior, there is a 95% probability that the true rate is in that range. And there is a 93.3% probability that the true rate is above the old 25%. That is a direct answer to 'is the new page better?'","out":"95% credible interval: 0.221 to 0.506\nP(rate > 25%) = 0.933","figs":[]},{"title":"The frequentist answer for comparison","text":"The usual approach tests the null value 25% and builds a confidence interval (Wilson). The numbers look alike, but the sentences mean different things: the confidence interval is about repeating the procedure, the credible interval about this rate.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nci = proportion_confint(k, n, alpha=0.05, method='wilson')\nse = np.sqrt(0.25 * 0.75 / n)\nz = (k / n - 0.25) / se\n\nprint(f'Wilson 95% CI: {ci[0]:.3f} to {ci[1]:.3f}')\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}, one-sided p = {stats.norm.sf(z):.3f}')","explain":["Load the function that builds a Wilson confidence interval for a proportion.","The Wilson 95% confidence interval for 14 out of 40, the frequentist range of plausible rates.","The standard error of a proportion if the true rate were the old 25%, the null value.","The z statistic: how many standard errors the observed 35% sits above 25%.","Print the Wilson confidence interval.","Print z and the one-sided p-value: the chance of a result this high if the true rate were only 25%."],"meaning":"The Wilson interval (0.221 to 0.505) is nearly the same as the credible interval, but it means something different: it describes how the procedure behaves over repeated samples. The z test gives z = 1.46 and a one-sided p = 0.072, not below 0.05, so we cannot reject 25%. That 0.072 is not the probability that the page is no better.","out":"Wilson 95% CI: 0.221 to 0.505\nz = 1.46, one-sided p = 0.072","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the prior and the posterior","text":"The flat prior is a level line. The data pull the belief into a peak near 0.35, and the shaded band is the 95% credible interval. The dashed line marks the old page's 25% rate.","code":"x = np.linspace(0, 1, 400)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, stats.beta(a, b).pdf(x), color='#7f7f7f', label='prior Beta(1, 1)')\nplt.plot(x, posterior.pdf(x), color='#1f77b4', label=f'posterior Beta({post_a}, {post_b})')\nmid = (x >= low) & (x <= high)\nplt.fill_between(x[mid], posterior.pdf(x[mid]), color='#1f77b4', alpha=0.2)\nplt.axvline(0.25, color='#d62728', linestyle='--', label='old rate 25%')\nplt.xlabel('Conversion rate')\nplt.ylabel('Density')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","explain":["Make 400 evenly spaced rates from 0 to 1 to draw the curves on.","Start a new figure that fits the page.","Draw the flat prior as a grey line.","Draw the posterior in blue; the label includes its two numbers.","Pick out the rates inside the 95% credible interval.","Shade the area under the posterior over those rates, the credible interval.","Draw a dashed red line at the old page's 25% rate.","Label the horizontal axis.","Label the vertical axis.","Add the legend that names the curves.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"The grey flat prior is a level line. The data pull the belief into a blue peak near 0.35, and the shaded band is the 95% credible interval. Most of the shaded area lies to the right of the dashed 25% line, which is why the probability that the rate is above 25% is high (0.933).","out":"","figs":["9.3-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"qbeta(c(0.025, 0.975), 15, 27)\n1 - pbeta(0.25, 15, 27)\n15 / (15 + 27)\nprop.test(14, 40, correct = FALSE)$conf.int\nprop.test(14, 40, p = 0.25, alternative = \"greater\", correct = FALSE)$p.value","expect":"credible interval 0.2212 0.5059; P(rate > 0.25) = 0.9334; posterior mean 0.3571; Wilson 95% CI 0.2213 0.5049; one-sided p-value = 0.0721","note":"prop.test with correct = FALSE gives the Wilson score interval and the plain z test, matching the Python. The Bayesian numbers come straight from the Beta distribution with qbeta and pbeta.","explain":["qbeta finds the 2.5% and 97.5% points of the Beta(15, 27) posterior, the 95% credible interval.","pbeta gives the posterior probability below 0.25; subtracting from 1 gives P(rate > 0.25).","The posterior mean of a Beta(a, b) is a / (a + b) = 15 / 42.","prop.test gives a Wilson-type confidence interval; correct = FALSE turns off the continuity correction.","A one-sided z test of the null rate 0.25; alternative = \"greater\" tests whether the true rate is above it, and $p.value extracts the p-value."],"read":"The first line gives the credible interval 0.2212 to 0.5059. The second gives P(rate > 0.25) = 0.9334. The third gives the posterior mean 0.3571. The fourth gives the Wilson interval 0.2213 to 0.5049. The last gives the one-sided p-value 0.0721, not below 0.05."},"spss":{"menu":"SPSS has no standard menu for Bayesian proportions, so use Transform > Compute Variable with the IDF.BETA and CDF.BETA functions","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE lower = IDF.BETA(0.025, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE upper = IDF.BETA(0.975, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE p_better = 1 - CDF.BETA(0.25, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE z = (0.35 - 0.25) / SQRT(0.25 * 0.75 / 40).\nCOMPUTE p_onesided = 1 - CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nFORMATS lower upper p_better z p_onesided (F8.4).\nLIST lower upper p_better z p_onesided.","note":"Bayesian analysis is not a standard menu in SPSS, so the Beta(15, 27) posterior is calculated directly (the 15 and 27 come from the flat prior plus 14 buyers and 26 non-buyers). The Wilson confidence interval is not a standard SPSS output, so only the z test is shown.","explain":["Create a data file with one dummy variable, because SPSS needs a case to compute on.","Marks the start of the data typed into the syntax.","A single row with the value 1.","Marks the end of the data.","Lower end of the 95% credible interval: the 2.5% point of Beta(15, 27). IDF.BETA is the inverse Beta CDF.","Upper end of the credible interval: the 97.5% point.","P(rate > 0.25) is 1 minus the Beta CDF at 0.25.","The z statistic for the frequentist test: (0.35 - 0.25) / the standard error at the old rate.","One-sided p-value: 1 minus the normal CDF at z.","Show each result with 4 decimals.","Print the results."],"read":"LIST shows lower .2212, upper .5059, p_better .9334, z 1.4606 and p_onesided .0721. So the credible interval is 0.221 to 0.506, there is a 93% posterior probability that the rate exceeds 25%, and the frequentist one-sided p is 0.072."},"stata":{"code":"display \"posterior mean = \" 15 / 42\ndisplay \"lower 95% = \" invibeta(15, 27, 0.025)\ndisplay \"upper 95% = \" invibeta(15, 27, 0.975)\ndisplay \"P(rate > 0.25) = \" 1 - ibeta(15, 27, 0.25)\ncii proportions 40 14, wilson\nprtesti 40 14 0.25","note":"invibeta(a, b, p) is the inverse Beta CDF and ibeta(a, b, x) the Beta CDF. cii proportions with the wilson option gives the Wilson interval, and prtesti runs the z test (read the Ha: p > 0.25 line, p about 0.072). These commands need no data in memory.","explain":["Display the posterior mean 15 / 42, the best single estimate of the rate.","invibeta is the inverse Beta CDF; this is the 2.5% point, the lower end of the 95% credible interval.","The 97.5% point, the upper end of the credible interval.","ibeta is the Beta CDF, so 1 minus it is the posterior probability that the rate is above 0.25.","cii proportions gives a confidence interval from 40 trials and 14 successes; wilson picks the Wilson method.","prtesti runs a one-sample z test of 14 out of 40 against the null rate 0.25."],"read":"The first four lines show .3571, .2212, .5059 and .9334. cii gives a Wilson interval of about .2213 to .5049. In prtesti, read the line Ha: p > 0.25: z = 1.4606 and Pr(Z > z) = 0.0721."},"conclusion":"Given 14 buyers out of 40 and a flat prior, the true conversion rate is probably between 22% and 51% (95% credible interval), and there is about a 93% probability that it beats the old 25%. The usual z test gives p = 0.072, which does not reach 0.05. Caution: 40 customers is a small sample, and the answer depends on the prior, so we should collect more data.","practice":{"scenario":"A marketing team tests a new email subject line on 30 subscribers and 9 click. The old subject line was clicked 20% of the time. Use a flat Beta(1, 1) prior and then a sceptical Beta(2, 8) prior that reflects the old 20% rate. How do the answers differ?","data":"k, n = 9, 30   # clicks and subscribers\npriors = [(1, 1), (2, 8)]   # (a, b) for the flat and sceptical priors\nold_rate = 0.2","tasks":["For each prior, find the posterior Beta distribution and its mean.","Find the 95% credible interval.","Find the probability that the true click rate is above 0.2.","How much does the prior matter with 30 subscribers?"],"hint":"The posterior is Beta(a + k, b + n - k). Use stats.beta(...).ppf([0.025, 0.975]) for the interval and .sf(0.2) for P(rate > 0.2).","solution":{"code":"from scipy import stats\n\nk, n = 9, 30\nfor a, b in [(1, 1), (2, 8)]:\n    post = stats.beta(a + k, b + n - k)\n    lo, hi = post.ppf([0.025, 0.975])\n    print(f'prior Beta({a},{b}): posterior mean {post.mean():.3f}, 95% interval {lo:.3f} to {hi:.3f}, P(rate > 0.2) = {post.sf(0.2):.3f}')","answer":"With the flat prior the posterior is Beta(10, 22): mean 0.312, 95% credible interval 0.167 to 0.480, and P(rate > 0.2) = 0.925. With the sceptical Beta(2, 8) prior the mean is 0.275, the interval 0.150 to 0.421 and P(rate > 0.2) = 0.859. The prior pulls the answer toward 20%. With 30 subscribers it matters noticeably, though both priors say the new line is probably better than 20%.","out":"prior Beta(1,1): posterior mean 0.312, 95% interval 0.167 to 0.480, P(rate > 0.2) = 0.925\nprior Beta(2,8): posterior mean 0.275, 95% interval 0.150 to 0.421, P(rate > 0.2) = 0.859","figs":[]}}});
